WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.577 position:50% size:92% align:center
The starter alternates positive two point zero

00:00:03.577 --> 00:00:06.424 position:50% size:92% align:center
and negative two point zero across one warp.

00:00:06.644 --> 00:00:09.280 position:50% size:92% align:center
The positive path doubles its value. The

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.378 position:50% size:92% align:center
negative path negates it. The question is which

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:14.751 position:50% size:92% align:center
lanes participate in each operation.

00:00:16.771 --> 00:00:19.566 position:50% size:92% align:center
A lane identifies a thread’s position in its

00:00:19.566 --> 00:00:22.551 position:50% size:92% align:center
warp. These positions do not represent separate

00:00:22.551 --> 00:00:25.283 position:50% size:92% align:center
physical processors owned by those threads.

00:00:25.503 --> 00:00:28.208 position:50% size:92% align:center
A warp instruction can have only a subset of

00:00:28.208 --> 00:00:29.684 position:50% size:92% align:center
its lanes participating.

00:00:31.704 --> 00:00:34.638 position:50% size:92% align:center
Sixteen lanes have positive inputs. Those lanes

00:00:34.638 --> 00:00:37.260 position:50% size:92% align:center
participate in the doubling operation; the

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.382 position:50% size:92% align:center
negative lanes do not update their

00:00:39.382 --> 00:00:41.005 position:50% size:92% align:center
results through this path.

00:00:41.225 --> 00:00:44.101 position:50% size:92% align:center
The diagram shows participation, not a claim

00:00:44.101 --> 00:00:45.865 position:50% size:92% align:center
that the compiler must emit

00:00:45.865 --> 00:00:47.433 position:50% size:92% align:center
this exact branch order.

00:00:49.453 --> 00:00:52.676 position:50% size:92% align:center
The other sixteen lanes participate in negation.

00:00:52.676 --> 00:00:55.160 position:50% size:92% align:center
Their negative two point zero becomes

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.771 position:50% size:92% align:center
positive two point zero.

00:00:56.991 --> 00:00:59.760 position:50% size:92% align:center
The same warp still contains all thirty-two

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.821 position:50% size:92% align:center
threads. A lane inactive for one

00:01:01.821 --> 00:01:03.753 position:50% size:92% align:center
operation has not disappeared.

00:01:05.773 --> 00:01:08.815 position:50% size:92% align:center
After the paths, lane zero has four point zero

00:01:08.815 --> 00:01:11.724 position:50% size:92% align:center
and lane one has two point zero. Each thread

00:01:11.724 --> 00:01:13.773 position:50% size:92% align:center
writes its own output position.

00:01:13.993 --> 00:01:16.489 position:50% size:92% align:center
Both lane sets belong to the original warp.

00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:21.638 position:50% size:92% align:center
Now consider thirty-two positive inputs. Pause

00:01:21.638 --> 00:01:24.087 position:50% size:92% align:center
and predict which lane set needs the

00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:25.720 position:50% size:92% align:center
negative-path operation.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.500 position:50% size:92% align:center
Does changing the signs change the warp’s size?

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.791 position:50% size:92% align:center
No lane needs negation in that case. All

00:01:36.791 --> 00:01:39.695 position:50% size:92% align:center
thirty-two can participate in doubling, and the

00:01:39.695 --> 00:01:42.043 position:50% size:92% align:center
warp is still thirty-two threads wide.

00:01:42.263 --> 00:01:45.521 position:50% size:92% align:center
Input patterns change instruction participation.

00:01:45.521 --> 00:01:48.508 position:50% size:92% align:center
They do not tell us an exact runtime without

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:50.476 position:50% size:92% align:center
compiler and device evidence.
